How traffic is handled

Vercel runs the Django app as serverless functions. Each concurrent request can start its own function instance, so the platform scales out on its own.
The database is Neon PostgreSQL. In settings.py it is configured with dj_database_url.parse(...) and no CONN_MAX_AGE. That means each request opens and closes its own connection.
Sessions are stored in the database, as Django does by default. Each request makes one session read, one user read and then its own queries.
Where the risk is

Connection limits: 100 simultaneous requests means up to 100 simultaneous Postgres connections. Neon's direct endpoint allows only a limited number, depending on the plan and compute size. If you hit the limit, you get intermittent 500s.
Opening a connection costs time: it adds roughly 50–150 ms to every request.
Neon cold start: on the free tier, compute suspends when idle and the first request after that is slow, a few seconds.
Login itself: password hashing is deliberately CPU-heavy, around 100–300 ms per login. It is fine at this scale.
Checks and fixes

Check that DATABASE_URL in Vercel uses Neon's pooled host. The hostname should contain -pooler. This is the most important fix, because the pooler (PgBouncer) can handle thousands of client connections.
With the pooler, add conn_max_age=0 and DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS=True to the database settings. I can make that change.
Check Neon's autosuspend setting. On the free plan you can't turn it off, so the first visitor after idle time waits.
Dashboards run several queries per page. I've already used select_related and prefetch_related in most views, so there is no obvious N+1 problem. The admin dashboard and the mock progress panel are the heaviest pages. At your scale, with tens of students and a few admins, that is fine.
The AI mock interview calls Gemini. Those calls are bounded by Gemini's rate limits, not your database.
